# The ledger of `math`. Its floor, the thin binding of `math.h`, is the module
# `math/hero_math` since 2026-11-19, `math/libm` until that day: the coherence
# review renamed it to README § A2's `hero_<x>`, and a program that named the
# floor to call C as C answers names it so now. What `math` answers is the
# face's, and did not move.

capability const/circle-and-e  pi, twice pi, and the base of the natural logarithm
  heroes  math.PI, math.TAU, math.E
  go      Pi, E
  rust    f64::consts::PI, TAU, E
  python  pi, tau, e

capability const/roots-and-logs  the square root of 2, and the logarithms a program divides by
  heroes  math.SQRT_2, math.LN_2, math.LN_10, math.LOG2_E, math.LOG10_E
  go      Sqrt2, Ln2, Ln10, Log2E, Log10E
  rust    f64::consts::SQRT_2, LN_2, LN_10, LOG2_E, LOG10_E
  python  -

capability const/golden-and-euler  the golden ratio and Euler's constant, which no one operation gives
  heroes  math.PHI, math.EULER_GAMMA
  go      Phi
  rust    f64::consts::GOLDEN_RATIO, EULER_GAMMA
  python  -

capability const/derived  a constant that is one operation on another
  heroes  refused: each is one division, product or `sqrt` of a constant the package gives, and a name for every such quotient is a list with no end
  go      SqrtE, SqrtPi, SqrtPhi
  rust    f64::consts::FRAC_1_PI, FRAC_1_SQRT_2, FRAC_1_SQRT_2PI, FRAC_1_SQRT_3, FRAC_1_SQRT_5, FRAC_1_SQRT_PI, FRAC_2_PI, FRAC_2_SQRT_PI, FRAC_PI_2, FRAC_PI_3, FRAC_PI_4, FRAC_PI_6, FRAC_PI_8, LOG10_2, LOG2_10, SQRT_3, SQRT_5
  python  -

capability limits/infinity-and-nan  the infinity and a value that is not a number
  heroes  math.INF, math.NAN
  go      Inf, NaN
  rust    f64::INFINITY, NEG_INFINITY, NAN
  python  inf, nan
  note    minus infinity is `-math.INF`; Heroes has no exponent literal, so these are computed from the largest float

capability limits/float-range  the largest and the smallest float, and the gap after 1.0
  heroes  math.MAX_F64, math.SMALLEST_F64, math.SMALLEST_NORMAL_F64, math.EPSILON, math.MAX_F32, math.SMALLEST_F32
  go      MaxFloat64, SmallestNonzeroFloat64, MaxFloat32, SmallestNonzeroFloat32
  rust    f64::MAX, MIN, MIN_POSITIVE, EPSILON
  python  -
  note    Rust's `MIN` is `-math.MAX_F64`; its `MIN_POSITIVE` is the smallest normal one, Go's smallest is the subnormal, so both are named for what they are

capability limits/float-format  the parameters of the float format
  heroes  refused: they are the numbers of IEEE 754's binary64, which no platform Heroes runs on changes, and a program that needs one writes it
  go      -
  rust    f64::RADIX, MANTISSA_DIGITS, DIGITS, MAX_EXP, MIN_EXP, MAX_10_EXP, MIN_10_EXP, MAX_EXACT_INTEGER, MIN_EXACT_INTEGER, BITS
  python  -

capability limits/integer-range  the largest and the smallest value of every integer type
  heroes  math.MAX_I8, math.MIN_I8, math.MAX_I16, math.MIN_I16, math.MAX_I32, math.MIN_I32, math.MAX_I64, math.MIN_I64, math.MAX_U8, math.MAX_U16, math.MAX_U32, math.MAX_U64
  go      MaxInt8, MinInt8, MaxInt16, MinInt16, MaxInt32, MinInt32, MaxInt64, MinInt64, MaxInt, MinInt, MaxUint8, MaxUint16, MaxUint32, MaxUint64, MaxUint, bits.UintSize
  rust    i64::MAX, MIN, BITS, max_value, min_value, and the same of each width
  python  -
  note    each constant has its own type; an unsigned type's smallest is 0; Go's `int` is 64 bits on every platform Heroes runs on

